    end_line: 290
    content: "---\nname: computer-use\ndescription: >-\n  Browser automation, web browsing, interactive UI testing, DOM/console\n  inspection, and visual screenshot or video capture for any website or URL\n  using Playwright CLI (`playwright-cli`). Trigger this skill whenever the user\n  asks to open, browse, inspect, scrape, interact with, test, debug, screenshot,\n  or record any external website, reference URL, or the locally running web\n  application (`http://localhost:3000`), or when you need to verify rendered UI\n  behavior, animations, DOM state, or browser console/network errors.\n---\n\n# Computer Use & Browser Automation (`playwright-cli`)\n\nThis skill equips you to launch a headless Chromium browser inside the applet\ncontainer using `@playwright/cli` (`playwright-cli`) to open and interact with\n**any website or URL**\342\200\224including public websites (e.g., for design inspiration,\nresearch, or live reference inspection) as well as the locally running applet\n(`http://localhost:3000`). You can navigate pages, click and fill elements,\ninspect accessibility snapshots, console logs, and network requests, and capture\n`.png` screenshots or `.webm` screen recordings.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Command Quick Reference\n\nBoth `playwright-cli` and headless Chromium (`/ms-playwright`) are pre-installed\nin the container image, and `.playwright-cli/` is ignored by default. You can\nrun these commands directly without any setup step:\n\n```bash\n# Session & navigation\nplaywright-cli open --browser=chromium http://localhost:3000\nplaywright-cli goto http://localhost:3000/settings\nplaywright-cli reload\nplaywright-cli resize 1440 900\nplaywright-cli close\nplaywright-cli kill-all\n\n# Snapshots & DOM inspection (returns accessibility tree with [ref=eN] handles)\nplaywright-cli snapshot\nplaywright-cli snapshot --depth=4\nplaywright-cli find \"Add to cart\"\nplaywright-cli find --regex \"/sign (in|up)/i\"\nplaywright-cli eval \"document.title\"\nplaywright-cli eval \"el => el.textContent\" e5\n\n# Interactions (target by snapshot ref eN, CSS selector, or role locator)\nplaywright-cli click e3\nplaywright-cli dblclick e7\nplaywright-cli fill e5 \"user@example.com\"\nplaywright-cli fill e5 \"search query\" --submit\nplaywright-cli type \"raw keyboard text\"\nplaywright-cli press Enter\nplaywright-cli select e9 \"option-value\"\nplaywright-cli check e12\nplaywright-cli uncheck e12\nplaywright-cli hover e4\nplaywright-cli dialog-accept\n\n# Captures & DevTools\nplaywright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png\nplaywright-cli video-start .playwright-cli/clip.webm\nplaywright-cli video-stop\nplaywright-cli console\nplaywright-cli requests\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Bundled Playwright CLI References (On-Demand Only)\n\nDo **not** stage or read these guides before writing application code. Only if\nyour task requires one of the specialized workflows below, stage the bundled\nreferences into `.playwright-cli/skill` in the shell first:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p .playwright-cli && rm -rf .playwright-cli/skill && \\\n  cp -r /ms-playwright/skill .playwright-cli/skill\n```\n\nThen read the corresponding guide under `/.playwright-cli/skill/references/`:\n\n-   `element-attributes.md` \342\200\224 Inspecting DOM attributes, classes, and computed\n    styles not visible in snapshots.\n-   `request-mocking.md` \342\200\224 Mocking and intercepting network traffic (`route` /\n    `unroute`).\n-   `running-code.md` \342\200\224 Running custom multi-step Playwright scripts via\n    `run-code`.\n-   `session-management.md` \342\200\224 Managing isolated or persistent browser sessions\n    (`-s=`, `list`, `close-all`, `kill-all`).\n-   `storage-state.md` \342\200\224 Saving and loading cookies, `localStorage`,\n    `sessionStorage`, and auth state.\n-   `tracing.md` \342\200\224 Capturing execution traces (`tracing-start` /\n    `tracing-stop`).\n-   `video-recording.md` \342\200\224 Recording browser sessions, chapters, and overlays\n    (`video-start` / `video-stop` and `page.screencast`).\n-   `playwright-tests.md` & `test-generation.md` \342\200\224 Running, debugging,\n    generating, and healing Playwright tests.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## AI Studio Container Rules\n\nWhen using `playwright-cli`, always apply these container-specific rules:\n\n1.  **Invoke the global `playwright-cli` binary directly (never `bunx` or\n    `npx`)**: `playwright-cli` is pre-installed on the `PATH`, so run commands\n    as `playwright-cli <command>`. **Never** run `bunx playwright-cli` or\n    `npx playwright-cli` (the unscoped `playwright-cli` npm package is a\n    different, unrelated package), **never** install `@playwright/cli` or\n    browser binaries (`bun add`, `npm install`, or `install-browser`), **never**\n    add `@playwright/cli` to the app\'s `package.json`, and **never** write\n    standalone Node scripts that import `playwright` (`chromium.launch()`).\n2.  **Always use headless Chromium (`--browser=chromium` or default `open`),\n    never `--browser=chrome` or `--headed`**: Only headless Chromium\n    (`/ms-playwright/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell`) is\n    installed in the container. Start sessions with `playwright-cli open <url>`\n    (or `playwright-cli open --browser=chromium <url>`, both of which use the\n    container\'s preconfigured `~/.playwright/cli.config.json`). **Never** pass\n    `--browser=chrome`, `--browser=firefox`, `--browser=webkit`, or\n    `--browser=msedge` (even though they appear in the bundled `SKILL.md`\n    examples\342\200\224system Google Chrome at `/opt/google/chrome/chrome` is not\n    installed), and **never** use `--headed` or GUI-only commands like `show\n    --annotate`.\n3.  **Pass relative paths to CLI flags, and read files relative to root `/`**:\n    -   In shell commands, always pass paths **relative to the project root**\n        (no leading `/`), inside `.playwright-cli/` (e.g.,\n        `--filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png`,\n        `.playwright-cli/clip.webm`, `.playwright-cli/dom.yaml`). Writing to\n        `/...` in the shell writes to the OS root outside the project, and\n        writing to `.` pollutes the user\'s application files.\n    -   Captures under `.playwright-cli/` from foreground commands are attached\n        or inlined automatically (see Rule 5). Whenever reading a file directly\n        (such as copied skill docs, artifacts outside `.playwright-cli/`, or\n        captures from a backgrounded command), the project directory is rooted\n        at `/`\342\200\224always prepend `/` to the project-relative path (e.g., read\n        `/.playwright-cli/skill/SKILL.md`), and **never** prepend the shell\'s\n        `pwd` or `realpath` output.\n4.  **Store user-requested deliverables in\n    `.aistudio/artifacts/computer_use/`**: Because `.playwright-cli/` is\n    gitignored, files inside it are hidden from the user\'s file explorer. Only\n    when the user explicitly asks you to save a screenshot or recording for them\n    to view/download, pass `.aistudio/artifacts/computer_use/<name>` to the CLI\n    (`mkdir -p .aistudio/artifacts/computer_use`) and read it at\n    `/.aistudio/artifacts/computer_use/<name>`. For your own validation, always\n    keep files in `.playwright-cli/`.\n5.  **Choose the right inspection modality (`snapshot`, `screenshot`, `video`)\n    and inspect every capture**: When a foreground `playwright-cli` shell\n    command finishes, `.playwright-cli/` screenshots and `.webm` videos\n    referenced in its output are **attached directly** to the command response,\n    and the latest `.playwright-cli/page-*.yml` snapshot plus new\n    `.playwright-cli/console-*.log` entries are **inlined automatically**.\n    Review them directly from the command response without reading those files\n    again (read `/.playwright-cli/<name>` manually only if the command finished\n    in the background):\n\n    -   **DOM & text state (`snapshot`)**: Use `playwright-cli snapshot` (or\n        `find`) for fast, low-token inspection of text content, DOM structure,\n        and `eN` element refs; the latest snapshot and new console log lines are\n        inlined in the command output. Target controls either using CSS\n        selectors, IDs, or accessible locators known from the code you wrote, or\n        using `eN` references from a prior page snapshot (returned by `open`,\n        interactions like `click`/`fill`, or `snapshot`) \342\200\224 **never** guess or\n        invent `eN` references before seeing a snapshot (e.g., never chain\n        `open` or a DOM-changing action with `click e7` in the same shell\n        command before reading the snapshot).\n    -   **Static visual layout (`screenshot`, unique filenames)**: When\n        verifying visual design, layout, styling, responsiveness (e.g. after\n        `resize`), or static canvas/3D rendering, capture a `.png` screenshot\n        with a **unique filename per capture** (e.g.,\n        `playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png`).\n        It is attached directly to the command response. **Never** overwrite a\n        screenshot path from an earlier capture, and keep screenshots bounded\n        (at most 4 per turn, using `snapshot` for intermediate DOM checks).\n    -   **Animations, scrolling, & time-based behavior (`video-start` /\n        `video-stop`)**: When verifying animations, transitions, scroll effects,\n        interactive gestures, physics/collisions, canvas/3D loops, or UI that\n        updates over time, record a `.webm` video inside `.playwright-cli/`\342\200\224when\n        `video-stop` finishes in the foreground, the clip is attached directly\n        to the command response:\n\n        ```bash\n        playwright-cli video-start .playwright-cli/clip.webm\n        # Perform interactions, scroll (mousewheel), or sleep while UI updates\n        playwright-cli mousewheel 0 500\n        sleep 2\n        playwright-cli video-stop\n        ```\n\n        For scripted pauses or visual callout overlays during recording, see\n        `references/video-recording.md` (under **Bundled Playwright CLI\n        References** above).\n\n6.  **Run commands sequentially, wait when backgrounded, and always trap `close`\n    on exit**:\n\n    -   Commands targeting the same browser session (the default session or a\n        named `-s=<name>` session) share a live browser instance. **Never**\n        start a follow-up command (`screenshot`, `snapshot`, `click`) while a\n        previous command on that session (`open`, `goto`, `video-stop`) is still\n        running in the background.\n    -   If a command is sent to the background, **stop calling tools and wait**\n        for the completion notification\342\200\224never poll background task status in a\n        loop, and **never** attempt to read a screenshot or snapshot file before\n        the background command has finished writing it.\n    -   **Always close the browser (`playwright-cli close`)** once visual\n        verification is complete so background Chromium processes do not keep\n        rendering WebGL/canvas loops and exhaust container CPU/memory while you\n        edit code. When chaining multiple steps in a single shell command,\n        **always register `trap \'playwright-cli close\' EXIT` at the top** (never\n        gate `close` behind `&&`, which skips closing if an earlier step fails\n        or times out):\n\n        ```bash\n        trap \'playwright-cli close\' EXIT\n        mkdir -p .playwright-cli && \\\n          playwright-cli open --browser=chromium http://localhost:3000 && \\\n          playwright-cli resize 1440 900 && \\\n          playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png\n        ```\n\n7.  **Restart the dev server after code edits (`DISABLE_HMR=true`)**: Hot Module\n    Replacement is disabled in the container. After editing application code,\n    restart the dev server before running `playwright-cli reload` or re-opening\n    `http://localhost:3000` so you do not inspect stale code.\n\n8.  **Headless WebGL/Canvas performance & `screenshot` timeout recovery**:\n\n    -   Headless Chromium here renders WebGL on the CPU (SwiftShader; no GPU).\n        If `open` or `screenshot` stalls on a WebGL / Three.js app, the app is\n        rendering back to back: switch it to the software-WebGL path in the\n        `threejs` skill (on-demand rendering, `afterDraw()` game loop) instead\n        of retrying the capture.\n    -   The container\'s `~/.playwright/cli.config.json` configures a 20,000ms\n        action timeout (`\"timeouts\": { \"action\": 20000 }`) for `playwright-cli\n        screenshot` (`screenshot` **does not accept a `--timeout` flag**).\n        Prefer `playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/view.png`\n        directly. If `screenshot` still times out on a heavy WebGL/Three.js\n        canvas, use `run-code` with a single positional `(page)` argument (not\n        `{ page }` and not a bare `await` statement):\n\n        ```bash\n        playwright-cli run-code \'async (page) => {\n          await page.screenshot({\n            path: \".playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png\",\n            timeout: 20000,\n          });\n        }\'\n        ```\n\n    -   If `open` or `screenshot` hangs or fails, check `playwright-cli console`\n        for runtime errors, and run `playwright-cli kill-all` before retrying\n        `open` so orphaned Chromium processes do not exhaust container memory.\n\n9.  **Drive canvas/WebGL content with coordinate and keyboard input, not locator\n    clicks**:\n\n    -   Locator clicks (`playwright-cli click <ref>` or `getByRole(...).click()`\n        inside `run-code`) wait until the target is actionable and pending\n        navigations settle. On a busy, software-rendered `requestAnimationFrame`\n        loop these waits can stall until they time out (30s inside `run-code`).\n        Canvas internals also have no snapshot refs.\n    -   For gameplay and other input inside a `<canvas>`, use the raw input\n        commands. They dispatch events immediately without actionability checks:\n        `mousemove <x> <y>`, `mousedown [button]`, `mouseup [button]`,\n        `mousewheel <dx> <dy>`, `press <key>`, `keydown <key>`, and `keyup\n        <key>`.\n    -   Coordinates are viewport CSS pixels. Read the canvas bounds first:\n\n        ```bash\n        playwright-cli eval \"() => JSON.stringify(\n          document.querySelector(\'canvas\').getBoundingClientRect())\"\n        ```\n\n    -   To check held input (movement, aiming, charging), hold the key or\n        button, capture while it is held, then release:\n\n        ```bash\n        trap \'playwright-cli close\' EXIT\n        playwright-cli open http://localhost:3000 && \\\n          playwright-cli mousemove 640 400 && \\\n          playwright-cli mousedown && playwright-cli mouseup && \\\n          playwright-cli keydown ArrowRight && sleep 1 && \\\n          playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/hold.png\n        playwright-cli keyup ArrowRight\n        ```\n\n    -   Keep ref-based `click` for regular DOM controls (menus, buttons, and\n        forms, including HTML overlays on top of a canvas).\n"
    num_lines: 291
